This page sets up the self-hosted editor for editors who are not developers. It is one Docker container: the image `ghcr.io/micacms/mica`. If you only edit on your own machine, you do not need this page. Use [Getting started](/docs/getting-started/).

> Mica is pre-1.0. Test upgrades and keep the version fixed in production.

Mica keeps its own Git clone of your content repository on its data volume. A save is a file in that clone that Git has not committed yet. **Publish** commits the chosen files as one commit and pushes it over HTTPS. The push starts your normal site build. There is no database, no content API, no read token, and no build webhook. Mica uses no API of your Git host: it uses Git over HTTPS.

To see it run first, [`deploy/demo-identity`](https://github.com/micacms/mica/tree/main/deploy/demo-identity) starts Mica and an identity provider on your machine with two commands.

## What you need

- A server with Docker and Docker Compose, and a reverse proxy that gives you HTTPS. Example: `https://cms.example.com`.
- A content repository on a Git host that you reach over HTTPS. It must contain the content model at `content/.mica/schema.json`. Your Astro build writes that file, and you commit it.
- An OpenID Connect provider. Mica keeps no passwords. If you have none, use Google: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/).

The Git hosts:

- **Supported, tested every week:** github.com and gitlab.com.
- **Tested in CI:** Forgejo.
- **Expected to work, not tested:** Bitbucket, Gitea, GitHub Enterprise Server, and self-managed GitLab.

Any Git host that serves HTTPS and accepts a token as the password should work. On a host that is not tested, run the check and one real publication before editors depend on it. A host with a private certificate authority is not supported yet.

Before the first start, do these steps in this order. The self-hosted editor clones the branch of your repository. It needs the model in that branch:

1. Build the site once (`npx astro build`). This writes `content/.mica/schema.json`.
2. Commit the content root, `content/`, with `.mica/schema.json`. Push it to the branch that Mica uses. A model without the content gives an editor with empty collections.
3. Make the token (step 2 below).
4. Run the check: `docker compose run --rm mica check`. It is described in "The check command" below. Fix each error that it reports.
5. Start Mica (step 6 below), sign in, and open each collection once.

SSH remotes are not supported yet. Git LFS under the content root is refused for now. To edit a checkout on your machine, use the local editor.

## 1. Get the two files

You do not clone the Mica repository. The image is published.

```bash
mkdir -p /srv/mica && cd /srv/mica
curl -o compose.yml https://raw.githubusercontent.com/micacms/mica/main/deploy/self-hosted/compose.yml
curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/micacms/mica/main/deploy/self-hosted/.env.example
```

## 2. Connect the repository

Mica clones your repository and pushes to it over HTTPS. Make a token for that one repository.

For GitHub, make a fine-grained personal access token for that one repository with **Contents: read and write**. Give it nothing else. In GitHub: Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → Fine-grained tokens.

For GitLab, make a project access token with the scopes **`read_repository`** and **`write_repository`**. Mica does not use the GitLab API, so it does not need the `api` scope. Give the token a role that may push to the branch. GitLab protects the default branch, and the **Developer** role cannot push to a protected branch. Do one of these:

- Give the token the **Maintainer** role.
- Let Developers push to the branch: Settings → Repository → Protected branches.
- Use a branch that is not protected.

Use a project access token, not a personal one, and give it a short expiry.

```env
MICA_REPOSITORY_URL=https://github.com/acme/website.git
MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN=github_pat_...
MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH=main
```

- `MICA_REPOSITORY_URL` is the HTTPS clone URL. Do not put credentials in it: Mica refuses `https://user:token@host/…`. Plain `http://` works only with a loopback host, for tests.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN` is the password for Git over HTTPS. Mica gives it only to this host and never writes it into the clone or a log. Mica reads it at the start, so a new token needs a restart. With `MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN_FILE` instead, Mica reads the file for each Git call, and a new token needs no restart.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH` defaults to `main`.
- The content root is the directory in the repository with the `.mica/schema.json`, such as `content`. When the repository has more than one, set `MICA_CONTENT_PATH` to the one that Mica edits. Mica reads and writes only below the root, even if the token can write more. Thus nobody can use the token through Mica to change the website's source, its CI configuration, or its dependencies. The token itself can: see "What the repository token can reach" in [Security](/docs/security/).
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_USERNAME` is optional. The default depends on the host: `x-access-token` for GitHub, `oauth2` for GitLab, and `mica` for other hosts.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_SYNC_SECONDS` is optional. It sets how often Mica fetches the branch. The default is 60.
- `MICA_DATA_DIR` is optional. It sets where Mica keeps its clone: in `MICA_DATA_DIR/repository`. The image uses the `/data` volume.

## 3. Connect the sign-in

Mica asks an OpenID Connect provider who a person is.

```env
MICA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://auth.example.com
MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=mica
MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
```

- `MICA_OIDC_ISSUER` must equal the `issuer` that the provider publishes at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`.
- Omit `MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET` for a public client that uses PKCE.
- Register one redirect URI with the provider: `<MICA_PUBLIC_URL>/api/auth/callback`.
- Mica asks for the scopes `openid profile email`. PKCE, state, and nonce are verified. The email address identifies an editor to the other editors.

Step by step: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/) or [Other sign-in providers](/docs/other-providers/).

## 4. Say who may edit

Your provider knows many people. It does not know who may edit this website. Set at least one rule. Mica refuses to start without one.

```env
MICA_ADMIT_GROUPS=mica-editors
MICA_ADMIT_DOMAINS=example.com
MICA_ADMIT_EMAILS=alice@gmail.com,bob@example.com
MICA_ADMIT_SUBJECTS=https://auth.example.com|a1b2c3
```

Each rule is a comma-separated list. One rule is enough.

- **Group:** the usual choice. Put a person in the group at your provider to add an editor. The claim name is `groups`. If your provider uses another name, set `MICA_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM`. A Keycloak path such as `/mica-editors` and the bare name both match. Google sends no groups claim.
- **Domain:** every address in a domain, but only addresses that the provider verified. Mica refuses a public domain such as `gmail.com` at start-up.
- **Emails:** these addresses, if the provider verified them. The comparison ignores case. Use this rule for Google accounts. One cost: if an organisation gives the address of a former editor to a new person, that person gets access. Gmail never reuses an address.
- **Subjects:** `issuer|subject` pairs. This is the strictest rule. A subject is never reassigned. The subject is the `sub` claim in the ID token. Your provider's console shows it as the user ID.

The email and domain rules need `email_verified` from the provider. If the provider does not send it, Mica refuses the person: an address that nobody verified is only what the person typed.

Everybody that a rule admits is an editor. There are no roles, no user table, and no invitations.

## 5. Set the address and the secret

```env
MICA_PUBLIC_URL=https://cms.example.com
MICA_TRUST_PROXY=true
MICA_SESSION_SECRET=
```

- `MICA_PUBLIC_URL` must be exactly what the browser shows, without a path. `https://` turns on Secure cookies. Mica allows plain-HTTP discovery only for a provider on this machine.
- Set `MICA_TRUST_PROXY=true` only behind a proxy that you control. Mica then uses the last address in `X-Forwarded-For`, which is the address that the proxy saw. This is correct for one proxy, such as Caddy or nginx. Behind a CDN and a proxy, all clients have the address of the CDN.
- `MICA_SESSION_SECRET` must have at least 32 characters, also for a trial. Make one:

```bash
openssl rand -base64 48
```

Each secret can also come from a file, such as a Docker secret: `MICA_SESSION_SECRET_FILE`, `MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE` and `MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN_FILE`. Mica reads the token file for each Git call, so a short-lived token can change without a restart.

[Configuration](/docs/configuration/) lists every variable, with its default.

The container listens on `127.0.0.1:6422`. Put TLS in front of it. With Caddy, this is enough:

```text
cms.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:6422
}
```

Publish answers after Git has pushed the commit. A large push, for example with new images, can take longer than a short proxy timeout. Set the read timeout of the proxy to a few minutes. In nginx, increase `proxy_read_timeout`.

Open only ports 80 and 443 in the firewall. Only the editors' browsers connect to Mica. The website build never connects to it.

## 6. Start and check

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose logs mica
curl -s https://cms.example.com/ready
```

You should see a start-up line that names the rule in force, and `/ready` should answer with `"status": "ok"`. Open `https://cms.example.com/admin/` and sign in. The System page shows the rule under "Who may edit".

At the first start, Mica clones the repository. Until the clone and the model are ready, `/ready` answers 503 with the reason `cloning`.

Mica stops at start-up, and lists every problem in one message, when:

- the repository URL or the token is missing, or the URL has credentials or is not HTTPS;
- it cannot make its first clone;
- the repository has no content model at `.mica/schema.json`;
- the content root uses Git LFS;
- the clone on the volume is for another URL or branch and holds drafts (the message names the files);
- no admission rule is set, or an old setting is in use.

When the token cannot push, Mica starts and logs a warning, and `check` names the cause. When a clone exists, a network or token problem does not stop Mica: editors can continue, the System page shows the sync error, and Mica tries again.

## The check command

`check` tells you if this configuration can serve the repository. It needs no browser, so you or a coding agent can run it from a shell:

```bash
docker compose run --rm mica check                   # before the first start
docker compose exec mica node dist/index.js check    # while Mica runs
```

It tests the configuration, read access, push rights with a dry run, the content model, Git LFS, and the data volume. It works in a temporary directory and never writes to the clone, so you can run it while Mica runs. A dry run does not see branch protection, server hooks, or other rules of the host. The first real publication shows such a refusal. [Command line](/docs/reference-cli/) gives the exit codes.

## Drafts, sync, and publish

A save is a draft. Drafts are files in the Git clone on the `/data` volume. All editors see them. On the Publish page, an editor chooses which saved changes to publish now, and the others stay saved. **Publish** commits the chosen changes as one commit: an `index.json`, its rich-text files, and its images together, so the repository never holds half an entry. The editor's summary ("Say what changed") is the commit message. The editor is the author of the commit. Mica (`Mica CMS <mica@localhost>`) is the committer, and the message ends with the trailer `Published-with: Mica`. Mica never forces a push.

Mica logs `Published` (with the commit and the number of files), `Discarded` (with the paths) and `Conflict resolved` (with the entry and the choice). Each line names the editor's email address. The content is never logged.

What Mica does with the repository:

- **It clones** the branch into `MICA_DATA_DIR/repository` at the first start. The clone has one branch, and Git downloads only the files of the current tree.
- **It starts** with the model in its clone, and syncs in the background. Only when the clone has no model that this version can read does the start wait for a sync.
- **It syncs** at once and then at each interval: it fetches the branch and fast-forwards. It never merges, rebases, or stashes. Developers can push to the branch as before. If a sync is still running at the next interval, Mica skips that interval.
- **It holds a version that it cannot use.** If the new version of the branch has a content model that this Mica cannot read (`model_unreadable`), or assigns Git LFS to files under the content root (`lfs_refused`), Mica keeps the old version. Editors can save, and Publish waits. Push a fix, or upgrade Mica.
- **It finds conflicts.** If somebody changes an entry on the website while an editor has a draft of that entry, this is a conflict. The editor sees it on the Overview, on the Publish page, and on the entry, and chooses **Keep my version** or **Use the website's version**. While a conflict exists, Mica does not fast-forward and Publish waits.
- **It tries again.** If the branch moved during a publication, Mica removes its commit, catches up, makes the commit again with the same author and message, and pushes. It tries three times. If the host cannot be reached, the publication stays committed in Mica, and the Publish page shows "Published in Mica, not on the website yet". Mica sends it later.
- **A refused push turns the publication back into drafts.** If the host refuses the push, for example because of a protected branch, a server hook, or a token that cannot write, the changes are saved drafts again. The Publish page and the System page show the reason of the host. Nothing is lost.
- **It uses a new model at once.** See "Upgrades and model changes" below.

The volume:

- Mount the volume at `MICA_DATA_DIR` (`/data` in the image), never at its `repository` folder. Mica makes a new clone next to the old one and renames it, and a mount point cannot be renamed.
- Run one Mica for each volume. A second Mica on the same volume waits up to 45 seconds for the first one to stop, then refuses to start. Deploy with the strategy "recreate", which stops the old container before it starts the new one, not with a rolling update. `docker compose up -d` does that.
- Make the volume about twice the size of the content: the objects in `.git` and the files. `.git` grows with each media change from the website. To make it smaller, publish or discard all drafts, stop Mica, and run `docker compose run --rm mica compact`. It makes a new clone with only the current files. It refuses a clone with drafts or with a publication that is not pushed yet.

The volume holds the clone and the unpublished drafts. If you lose it, unpublished work is gone. Nobody is signed out, and everything published is in the repository. Tell editors to publish often: the drafts are not an archive.

## Build the website

The build reads the repository. It needs no token, no CMS address, and no network access to Mica. Each Publish is a commit, and the push starts the build. A save starts nothing.

```yaml
# .github/workflows/build.yml
name: Build website
on:
  push:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with: { node-version: 24 }
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npx astro build
      # The model in the commit must be the one that the build wrote.
      - run: git diff --exit-code content/.mica/schema.json
      # Every entry must agree with the model (needs @micacms/editor as a dev dependency).
      - run: npx mica check
      # Then upload dist/ to your host.
```

If your host builds from the repository, for example Vercel, Netlify, or Cloudflare Pages, you do not need this workflow.

Editors commit more frequently than developers. To keep expensive CI jobs away from content commits, filter them with `paths-ignore: ["content/**"]`, and give the site build the opposite filter.

`MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH` can be any branch. A second Mica, with its own volume, on `MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH=staging` gives editors a staging site. You then review and merge that branch like any other change. Mica itself has no approval step.

## Sessions and removal of access

A session is an encrypted cookie. Mica keeps no list of sessions.

```env
MICA_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=8
MICA_SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=4
```

- A session ends after 8 hours, or after 4 hours without use. While a session is in use, Mica issues the cookie again every 5 minutes.
- Five minutes before a session ends, the editor warns and offers to stay signed in. When a session has ended, the page stays open and offers to sign in again. The entry and settings forms keep their unsaved changes in the browser tab (`sessionStorage`), and after the sign-in the form offers to restore them.
- Sign-out clears the cookie in the browser only. A copied cookie stays valid until it expires.
- Mica re-reads the rules on each request. The rules live in `.env`, so restart the container after a change. The change then counts at once.
- A change at your provider, for example removal from a group, counts from the next sign-in.
- To remove a person at once, take them out of the rules and restart. To end all sessions, change `MICA_SESSION_SECRET` and restart.

## Health

`GET /health` answers while the process runs. The image's health check uses it, so a problem at the Git host does not restart the container. `GET /ready` reports the clone: `status` is `degraded` when something needs a person, with reason codes such as `auth_failed` or `pending_push`, and `lastSyncAt`. It answers 503 only while there is no clone. Point an uptime monitor at `/ready`, and alert on `status`, not on the HTTP status code. `/ready` does not check the identity provider: a short outage of the provider must stop new sign-ins only. [Health endpoints](/docs/reference-health/) lists every code.

## Backups

The repository is the content backup, with the history of every publish. Any clone is a full copy. To keep your own copy, mirror it on a schedule:

```bash
git clone --mirror https://github.com/acme/website.git
git --git-dir=website.git remote update --prune
```

To restore, clone. There is nothing to import, and you do not need Mica to read the files.

For unpublished work, back up the `/data` volume. First publish all drafts, or stop the container. Then copy the volume. A copy made during a Git write can be inconsistent. For the drafts only, copy the content root of the clone: `MICA_DATA_DIR/repository/<content root>`.

Do not run `git fetch` or `git push` through `docker compose exec`: that shell has no credentials and none of Mica's isolation. Use it only for `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 git status` and `git diff`. Without that variable, `git status` can lock the index and make a Mica commit fail.

## Upgrades and model changes

The image tag in `compose.yml` is the version. Read the [changelog](https://github.com/micacms/mica/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md) first. Pin a version, not `latest`, so that an upgrade is a decision. Change the tag, then run:

```bash
docker compose pull && docker compose up -d
```

When you change the content model, run the Astro build, commit `.mica/schema.json`, and push. Mica brings the change in with its next sync and uses the new model at once; editors see a notice to load the page again. The image holds no model, so a model change never needs a new image. If this Mica cannot read the new model, it keeps the old version of the branch, and `/ready` has `model_unreadable`. Upgrade Mica, or push a model that it can read. A change to a `.gitattributes` file needs no restart.

## Verify the image

The image is built for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`. Docker pulls the one for your host. The license notices are in `/app/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt` for the server and in `/app/packages/editor-ui/dist/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt` for the editor.

From 0.4.0, each image has a keyless [cosign](https://docs.sigstore.dev/) signature from the release workflow of the Mica repository, an SBOM, and build provenance. To verify an image before you run it:

```bash
cosign verify ghcr.io/micacms/mica:<version> \
  --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github.com/micacms/mica/\.github/workflows/release\.yml@refs/tags/v' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com

docker buildx imagetools inspect ghcr.io/micacms/mica:<version> --format '{{ json .SBOM }}'
```

The compose file hardens the container: a read-only root file system, `/tmp` in memory, no Linux capabilities, `no-new-privileges`, and a 512 MB memory limit. Only the `/data` volume is writable.

Git in the image has settings for a large repository with many images and for a slow network. You cannot change them:

- A fetch or a push stops when it stays below 1 kB/s for 60 seconds, and after 5 minutes in all. The first clone can take 60 minutes.
- A repack uses one thread and at most 256 MB for its window (`pack.threads`, `pack.windowMemory`). Git does not compare files larger than 16 MB with other files (`core.bigFileThreshold`).
- `feature.manyFiles` and `core.untrackedCache` make the status of a large tree faster.

## Troubleshooting

The System page and the log show the message of the Git host, without the token.

**Mica stops at the start.** Read `docker compose logs mica`, and run `docker compose run --rm mica check`. The message lists each problem:

- "`MICA_REPOSITORY_URL` contains credentials": remove them, and set `MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN`.
- "must be an https:// URL": use the HTTPS URL. SSH is not supported yet.
- "assigns Git LFS to files under the content root": keep LFS out of the content root.
- "The clone on the data volume is for …": the branch changed, and the clone holds drafts. Start with the old branch and publish, or copy the drafts out and delete the volume. A new URL for the same repository needs nothing: Mica keeps the clone and fetches from the new URL.
- No content model: build the Astro project, commit `.mica/schema.json`, and push it to `MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH`.
- "MICA_SESSION_SECRET: is required" or "Nothing says who may edit this site": set `MICA_SESSION_SECRET` or an admission rule.

**The repository refused the token** (`/ready` has `auth_failed`). The token is wrong, expired, or revoked. Make a new token, put it in `.env`, and restart. If `MICA_REPOSITORY_USERNAME` is set to an empty value, remove it.

**The token can read but not write** (`/ready` has `write_forbidden`). On GitLab, add `write_repository`. On GitHub, give Contents: read and write.

**`check` passes, but a publication is refused.** A protected branch, a server hook, or required signed commits can cause this. The System page shows the reason of the host. On GitLab, use the Maintainer role, or let Developers push. The changes are drafts again: publish again after the fix.

**The repository does not exist, or the token cannot see it.** The URL is wrong, or the token is for a different repository. The host gives the same answer for both.

**The branch was rewritten** (a force push or a reset). Mica follows it at the next sync. Drafts stay drafts on the new version, and each of their entries shows as a conflict: the editor chooses to keep the draft or to take the website's version. A publication that never reached the host stays as drafts too. The log has "The branch was rewritten". After a restart, the drafts stay, but they no longer show as conflicts.

**The repository cannot be reached** (`/ready` has `network` or `sync_failing`). The host is down, DNS fails, or the container has no outgoing HTTPS. Run `curl -I <url>` on the server. Mica tries again at each sync.

**`/ready` stays at `cloning`.** The first clone cannot finish, because of the network or a very large repository. Read the log. A large clone can take minutes.

**"A newer content model cannot be read"** on the System page. The pushed `.mica/schema.json` is not valid, or it comes from a newer `@micacms/astro` than the image. Correct the model and push again, or upgrade the image.

**"Read-only in this version of Mica"** on a collection. The site uses a field type from a newer `@micacms/astro` than the image. Upgrade the image to that version or newer.

**"Some of these changes were edited while they were being published".** A colleague saved one of the files during the publication. Look at the changes again, then publish.

**The disk is full.** The clone grows with each media change. See "The volume" above, and run `compact`.

**"That account has no access to this site" after the sign-in.** The person signed in, but no rule admits them. Set `MICA_LOG_LEVEL=debug`, restart, and sign in again: the log gives the reason. Make sure that the groups claim arrives, or add the person to `MICA_ADMIT_EMAILS` or `MICA_ADMIT_SUBJECTS`.

**"That sign-in attempt expired or was not started here".** The browser lost the sign-in cookie. Usually `MICA_PUBLIC_URL` is not the address in the browser. Make them the same.

**"The identity provider could not be reached".** `MICA_OIDC_ISSUER` is wrong, or the container cannot reach the provider. Open `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration` from the container.

**The website shows old content.** The host built an older commit. Make sure that CI ran on the commit of the publication.

## Next

Sign in with a Google account: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/). Use another provider: [Other sign-in providers](/docs/other-providers/).
